1
00:00:01,806 --> 00:00:06,948
如果现代人工智能背后的最大假设是错误的，那会怎样？
What if the biggest assumption behind modern artificial intelligence is wrong?

2
00:00:07,502 --> 00:00:12,740
多年来，AI 行业一直在追求一个极其成功的公式。
For years, the AI industry has been pursuing a remarkably successful formula.

3
00:00:13,294 --> 00:00:21,680
更多的数据，更强的算力，更大的模型，而在这一切的核心，是一个看似简单的目标。
More data, more computing power, bigger models, and at the center of it all, a deceptively simple objective.

4
00:00:21,934 --> 00:00:23,524
预测下一个词。
Predict the next word.

5
00:00:23,822 --> 00:00:29,412
重复成千上万亿次，奇妙的事情就发生了。
Do this billions upon billions of times, and something extraordinary happens.

6
00:00:29,902 --> 00:00:37,764
模型开始写代码、解决数学问题、解释科学概念，甚至看起来像在进行推理。
The model starts writing code, solving mathematical problems, explaining scientific concepts, even appearing to reason.

7
00:00:38,190 --> 00:00:46,160
但学习预测对世界的描述，就意味着学会了世界真实的运作方式吗？
Does learning to predict descriptions of the world necessarily mean learning how the world actually works?

8
00:00:46,510 --> 00:00:51,300
想象一个读过所有物理教科书的 AI。
Imagine an AI that has read every physics textbook ever written.

9
00:00:51,662 --> 00:00:53,892
它可以完美地解释重力。
It can explain gravity perfectly.

10
00:00:54,254 --> 00:01:01,988
但这是否意味着它拥有了和看到玻璃从桌上掉落的孩子一样的物理认知？
But does that mean it has developed the same kind of physical understanding as a child who watches a glass fall off a table?

11
00:01:02,414 --> 00:01:11,172
在 2026 年 10 月，Randall Balestriero 和 Yann LeCun 发布了一篇提供全新视角的研究论文。
And in October 2026, Randall Balastriero and Jan Lacun released a research paper that offers a striking new perspective.

12
00:01:11,694 --> 00:01:18,820
一个只有 1 亿参数的模型，在特定测试中表现优于规模大出数百倍的模型。
A model with just 109 million parameters outperformed models hundreds of times larger on a particular test.

13
00:01:19,342 --> 00:01:25,316
这是指文本模型的内部世界观与独立训练的视觉模型之间的匹配程度。
How closely a text model's internal picture of the world matches that of an independently trained vision model.

14
00:01:25,646 --> 00:01:31,620
他们的研究表明，AI 的下一次飞跃可能并非仅仅来自把模型做大。
Their findings suggest that the next leap in AI might not come simply from making models bigger.

15
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:36,900
它可能源于从根本上重新思考我们训练它们去学习的内容。
It might come from fundamentally rethinking what we train them to learn.

16
00:01:37,230 --> 00:01:44,560
其影响涵盖了从类人推理到知识本质的各个方面。
And the implications extend from human-like reasoning all the way to the nature of knowledge itself.

17
00:01:54,126 --> 00:01:59,428
为了理解原因，让我们从人类智能几乎毫不费力就能做到的事情开始。
To understand why, let's start with something human intelligence does almost effortlessly.

18
00:01:59,694 --> 00:02:01,680
想象你手里拿着一个球，悬在地面上方。
Imagine holding a ball above the ground.

19
00:02:01,934 --> 00:02:03,524
你松开它，会发生什么？
You release it, what happens?

20
00:02:04,334 --> 00:02:04,964
它会掉下去。
It falls.

21
00:02:05,486 --> 00:02:09,187
现在，想象你在水下或月球上松开同一个球。
Now, imagine releasing that same ball underwater or on the moon.

22
00:02:09,582 --> 00:02:12,452
你脑子里有一个关于物体如何运动的内部模型。
You have an internal model of how objects behave.

23
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:18,160
关键在于，你可以利用这个模型去想象在不同条件下会发生什么。
And crucially, you can use that model to imagine what would happen under different conditions.

24
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:21,040
这是因果推理的一个基本组成部分。
That's a fundamental part of causal reasoning.

25
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:25,060
发育心理学中还有另一个有趣的线索。
There's another fascinating clue from developmental psychology.

26
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:38,240
即使是幼儿也会对物理对象产生预期，比如它们被藏起来时依然
存在，固体物体不能互相穿过，以及物体是沿连续路径运动的。
Even very young children develop expectations about physical objects, that they persist when hidden,
that solid objects cannot simply pass through each other, and that objects move in continuous paths.

27
00:02:38,542 --> 00:02:45,828
2022 年，DeepMind 的研究人员受这些想法启发，开发了一个名为 Plato 的系统。
In 2022, researchers at DeepMind developed a system called Plato, inspired by these ideas.

28
00:02:46,510 --> 00:02:56,400
它不再仅仅把视觉场景视为像素的集合，而是表征单个物体，并学习它们随时间变化的相互作用。
Instead of treating a visual scene simply as a collection of pixels, it
represented individual objects and learned about their interactions over time.

29
00:02:56,640 --> 00:03:08,036
它随后在简单模拟场景中测试了符合物理规律和不符合物理规律的事件，
并对几个基本的物理概念产生了敏感度。
It was then tested on physically plausible and impossible events in simple simulated scenes,
and it developed sensitivity to several fundamental physical concepts.

30
00:03:08,622 --> 00:03:20,720
也许通用智能不仅取决于看到足够的例子，还取决于建立有
用的内部构建块——即物体、关系以及支配它们变化的规则。
Perhaps general intelligence depends not just on seeing enough examples, but on developing useful internal building blocks, objects,
relationships, and the rules governing how they change.

31
00:03:21,070 --> 00:03:26,916
但 next token prediction 并不直接要求学习环境的真实因果模型。
But next token prediction doesn't directly require learning a faithful causal model of the environment.

32
00:03:27,374 --> 00:03:30,640
它奖励的是预测接下来的文本。
It rewards predicting the text that comes next.

33
00:03:35,918 --> 00:03:37,124
看看这个句子。
Consider this sentence.

34
00:03:37,550 --> 00:03:49,860
玻璃从桌子上掉下来，语言模型学到下一个词可能是“碎裂”，
这可能要求模型去学习关于重力、材料和碰撞的知识。
The glass fell from the table, and a language model learns that the next word might be shattered,
and making it may require the model to learn something about gravity, materials, and collisions.

35
00:03:50,414 --> 00:03:57,700
但它也可以通过学习到“玻璃掉落”这个短语经常跟着“破裂”或“碎裂”之类的词来成功。
But it can also succeed by learning that the phrase glass fell is frequently followed by words like broke or shattered.

36
00:03:57,998 --> 00:04:01,956
训练过程并没有明确区分这两种路径。
The training process doesn't explicitly distinguish those routes.

37
00:04:02,350 --> 00:04:06,820
要明确一点，说语言模型只是鹦鹉学舌是不准确的。
To be clear, it's a mistake to say language models are merely parrots.

38
00:04:07,118 --> 00:04:14,464
2023年，研究人员用棋盘游戏“黑白棋”的走法序列训练了一个 GPT 模型。
In 2023, researchers trained a GPT model on sequences of moves from the board game Othello.

39
00:04:14,784 --> 00:04:18,851
模型从未被明确给过棋盘的表示。
The model was never explicitly given a representation of the board.

40
00:04:19,149 --> 00:04:28,068
然而，当研究人员检查其内部激活状态时，他们发现模型已经产生了关于棋子位置的表示。
Yet when researchers examined its internal activations, they found evidence
that it had developed a representation of the positions of the pieces.

41
00:04:28,878 --> 00:04:36,004
而且当他们实验性地改变那个内部表示时，他们就能改变模型预测的走法。
And when they experimentally changed that internal representation, they could change the moves the model predicted.

42
00:04:36,526 --> 00:04:44,580
所以 next token 风格的预测确实能产生内部 world models，
至少对于像棋盘游戏这样简单且自给自足的世界来说是如此。
So next token style prediction can produce internal world models, at least for a simple, self-contained world like a board game.

43
00:04:44,878 --> 00:04:50,384
问题不在于它能不能做到，而在于有没有更直接、更高效的方法来实现这一点。
The question isn't whether it can, it's whether there's a more direct and efficient way to get there.

44
00:04:50,544 --> 00:05:02,244
我们花了多少计算资源在教 AI 去模拟描述现实的语言，而不是直接模拟现实本身内部的关系？
How much computation are we spending teaching AI to model the language used to describe reality,
rather than directly modeling the relationships within reality itself?

45
00:05:02,830 --> 00:05:06,564
这带我们进入一个有趣的哲学思想。
And this brings us to a fascinating philosophical idea.

46
00:05:12,064 --> 00:05:17,764
2000多年前，Plato 描述了被锁在洞穴里的囚犯。
Over 2,000 years ago, Plato described prisoners chained inside a cave.

47
00:05:18,126 --> 00:05:21,028
他们只能看到在墙上移动的影子。
All they could see were shadows moving across a wall.

48
00:05:21,294 --> 00:05:28,304
由于无法观察投射影子的物体，他们把影子误认为是现实本身。
Unable to observe the objects casting those shadows, they mistook the shadows for reality itself.

49
00:05:28,544 --> 00:05:32,224
现在想象三个人工智能。
Now imagine three artificial intelligences.

50
00:05:32,384 --> 00:05:35,332
一个完全从文字描述中学习。
One learns entirely from written descriptions.

51
00:05:35,566 --> 00:05:37,092
另一个从照片中学习。
Another learns from photographs.

52
00:05:37,646 --> 00:05:38,948
第三个从声音中学习。
A third learns from sounds.

53
00:05:39,438 --> 00:05:45,476
一只狗可以用像素表示，也可以用文字描述，或者通过吠叫声来识别。
A dog can be represented by pixels, described in words, or recognized by the sound of barking.

54
00:05:45,838 --> 00:05:47,344
观察到的信息是不同的。
The observations are different.

55
00:05:47,662 --> 00:05:49,700
但底层的动物是相同的。
The underlying animal is the same.

56
00:05:50,094 --> 00:05:55,364
2024年，研究人员提出了 Platonic Representation Hypothesis。
In 2024, researchers proposed the platonic representation hypothesis.

57
00:05:55,726 --> 00:06:12,384
这个想法是，随着神经网络变得越来越强大，它们的内部表示可能会越来越多地趋向于一个共享
的现实统计模型，即使它们是独立训练的，甚至即使它们是从完全不同的模态中学习的。
The idea was that as neural networks become more capable, their internal representations might increasingly converge towards a shared
statistical model of reality, even if they were trained independently, and even if they learned from entirely different modalities.

58
00:06:12,558 --> 00:06:14,340
但为什么这种收敛会发生？
But why would this convergence happen?

59
00:06:14,830 --> 00:06:18,436
而且真的需要构建越来越大的模型吗？
And did it really require building ever larger models?

60
00:06:18,958 --> 00:06:21,316
这篇新论文试图回答这两个问题。
The new paper attempts to answer both questions.

61
00:06:27,024 --> 00:06:34,404
研究人员关注一类被称为 JEPA 的架构，即联合嵌入预测架构。
The researchers focus on a family of architectures called Jeppas, joint embedding predictive architectures.

62
00:06:34,958 --> 00:06:43,556
JEPA 不是训练模型去重建观测中的每一个细节，而是学习内部表征之间的关系。
Rather than training a model to reconstruct every detail of an
observation, Jeppas learn relationships between internal representations.

63
00:06:44,064 --> 00:06:47,236
想象一下给 AI 展示「好视图」描述。
Imagine showing an AI2 descriptions.

64
00:06:47,726 --> 00:06:52,548
「一只狗在海滩上奔跑，一只小狗在海边玩耍。」
A dog is running along a beach, and a puppy is playing beside the sea.

65
00:06:52,942 --> 00:06:57,828
措辞不同，但这两句话可能在描述完全相同的场景。
The wording is different, but both sentences might describe the exact same scene.

66
00:06:58,222 --> 00:07:02,724
现在训练模型，让它为这两个描述生成相似的嵌入。
Now train the model to produce similar embeddings for both descriptions.

67
00:07:03,246 --> 00:07:07,172
嵌入只是一个拥有数百个维度的空间中的点。
An embedding is just a point in a space with hundreds of dimensions.

68
00:07:07,662 --> 00:07:17,984
研究人员称这类「好视图」为对同一场景的两个独立描述，
或者从不同角度拍摄的展示相同环境的两张照片。
The researchers call pairs like these good views two independent descriptions of the same scene,
or two photographs showing the same environment from different perspectives.

69
00:07:18,286 --> 00:07:27,104
对比一下，如果拿出一句完整的句子，把它拆成片
段，遮盖掉一些词，然后训练模型去关联这些片段。
Compare that with taking a single sentence, splitting it into fragments, masking some words,
and training the model to relate those fragments.

70
00:07:27,438 --> 00:07:31,268
这些片段仍然共享同一个作者的词汇和风格。
The fragments still share the same author's vocabulary and style.

71
00:07:31,566 --> 00:07:36,068
研究人员称这种训练方式为跨模态对齐中的「坏视图」。
The researchers call this kind of training a bad view for cross-modal alignment.

72
00:07:36,544 --> 00:07:44,868
并不是因为遮盖或 next token prediction 没用，
而是因为它们可能会保留仅属于单一观测的信息。
Not because masking or next token prediction is useless, but because it can preserve information peculiar to a single observation.

73
00:07:45,166 --> 00:07:47,620
接下来是核心的理论结果。
And then comes the central theoretical result.

74
00:07:47,984 --> 00:08:02,144
在特定的数学假设下，研究人员证明，如果分别训练的 JEPA 从世界状态之间的等效关系中学习，
它们的最佳表征在旋转变换下将是相同的。
Under specific mathematical assumptions, the researchers prove that if separately trained Jeppas learn from equivalent relationships
between world states, their optimal representations will be identical up to a rotation.

75
00:08:02,304 --> 00:08:06,224
想象两个人独立地绘制同一座城市的地图。
Think of two people independently drawing maps of the same city.

76
00:08:06,384 --> 00:08:15,716
他们的地图朝向可能不同，但如果街道和建筑之间的关系得到了保留，
旋转其中一张地图就可以让它与另一张对齐。
Their maps might face different directions, but if the relationships between the streets and buildings are preserved,
rotating one map can align it with the other.

77
00:08:16,142 --> 00:08:18,884
现在，想象在数百个维度中这样做。
Now, imagine doing that in hundreds of dimensions.

78
00:08:19,438 --> 00:08:23,104
一张地图是从图像中学习的，另一张是从文本中学习的。
One map learned from images, the other learned from text.

79
00:08:23,310 --> 00:08:26,864
研究人员称之为 Platonic Representation Theorem。
The researchers call this the Platonic Representation Theorem.

80
00:08:27,310 --> 00:08:41,216
它并不能证明每个神经网络都能学习到现实的真实结构，但它为独立训练的模型何时能收敛提供
了数学解释，并为如何促进这种收敛提供了实践线索。
It doesn't prove that every neural network learns the true structure of reality, but it gives a mathematical explanation for when
independently trained models can converge and a practical clue about how to encourage that convergence.

81
00:08:41,376 --> 00:08:43,056
但这里有一个关键的区别。
But there's a crucial distinction.

82
00:08:43,216 --> 00:08:49,456
两个模型可能在世界如何组织上达成共识，但并不理解是什么导致了变化。
Two models might agree on how the world is organized without understanding what makes it change.

83
00:08:49,742 --> 00:08:57,668
2021 年，Bernhard Shokoff、Yoshua Benjo 及其同
事提出了所谓的 causal representation learning。
In 2021, Bernhard Shokoff, Yoshua Benjo, and colleagues argued for something called causal representation learning.

84
00:08:58,030 --> 00:09:03,716
其目标是直接从原始观测中发现有意义的因果变量。
The ambition is to discover meaningful causal variables directly from raw observations.

85
00:09:04,238 --> 00:09:15,268
不仅是识别出玻璃杯和桌子存在，还要识别出位置、速度和接触等属性，
这些属性有助于解释当玻璃杯被推开时发生了什么。
Not just to recognize that a glass and a table exist, but to identify properties like position, velocity,
and contact that help explain what happens when a glass is pushed.

86
00:09:20,896 --> 00:09:26,628
回到那篇新论文，以及挑战当前主流 AI 开发方法的发现。
Back to the new paper and the finding that challenges the dominant approach to AI development.

87
00:09:27,056 --> 00:09:34,340
研究人员展示了普通的 next token prediction 可以写成类似于 JEPA 目标的形式。
The researchers show that ordinary next token prediction can be written in a form resembling a JEP objective.

88
00:09:34,862 --> 00:09:39,780
但它学习到的正相关关系是上下文与随后的 token 之间的关系。
But the positive relationship it learns is between a context and the token that follows it.

89
00:09:40,206 --> 00:09:42,276
两者都来自同一段文本序列。
Both come from the same sequence of text.

90
00:09:42,606 --> 00:09:52,292
这意味着训练目标自然地捕捉到了措辞和延续的模式，以及这些模式所编码的任何世界知识。
That means the training objective naturally captures patterns of wording and
continuation alongside whatever knowledge of the world those patterns encode.

91
00:09:52,810 --> 00:09:55,136
他们拥有 1.09 亿参数的 Goodview 模型。
Their 109 million parameter Goodview.

92
00:09:55,376 --> 00:09:59,830
JEPA 实现了 0.184 的对齐分数。
JEPA achieved an alignment score of 0.184.

93
00:09:59,830 --> 00:10:06,884
拥有 720 亿参数的模型实现了 0.154。
22.5 with 72 billion parameters achieved 0.154.

94
00:10:07,246 --> 00:10:12,976
拥有 700 亿参数的 Llama 3 达到了 0.151。
Llama 3 with 70 billion parameters reached 0.151.

95
00:10:13,262 --> 00:10:16,516
那个 0.184 是他们表现最好的训练结果。
That 0.184 was their best training run.

96
00:10:16,878 --> 00:10:25,216
四个运行周期的平均分是 0.168，仍高于他们测试过的所有语言模型。
Averaged across four runs, it scores 0.168, still above every language model they tested.

97
00:10:25,742 --> 00:10:38,656
使用相同的底层文本语料库，Goodview Jepa 在 2160 万训练样本后达到了与 next token
prediction 在 9 亿样本后大致相同的对齐水平。
Using the same underlying text corpus, the Goodview Jepa reached approximately the same alignment after 21.6 million training samples
that Next Token prediction reached after 900 million.

98
00:10:38,736 --> 00:10:46,016
这意味着训练样本减少了 42 倍，或者说文本 token 减少了约 14 倍。
That's 42 times fewer training samples, or approximately 14 times fewer text tokens.

99
00:10:46,830 --> 00:10:55,364
这里的启示是，你如何构建学习问题，会极大地改变模型获取特定类型理解的效率。
The lesson, how you structure the learning problem, can dramatically
change how efficiently a model acquires a particular kind of understanding.

100
00:10:55,694 --> 00:11:02,628
他们较小的模型并没有在通用推理、编程或对话方面击败那些巨型语言模型。
Their smaller model did not beat the giant language models at general reasoning, coding, or conversation.

101
00:11:02,926 --> 00:11:06,756
但在跨模态表征对齐的衡量指标上，它胜过了那些模型。
It beat them on a measure of cross-modal representation alignment.

102
00:11:07,182 --> 00:11:13,696
而且大规模训练数据中包含了由现有视觉语言系统生成的标注。
And the large-scale training data included captions generated by an existing vision language system.

103
00:11:13,966 --> 00:11:18,896
规模化可以让次优的学习目标变得极其强大。
Scaling can make a suboptimal learning objective extremely powerful.

104
00:11:19,118 --> 00:11:25,636
但选择更合适的客观目标，有时可以用更少的计算量更接近你的目标。
But choosing a more suitable objective may sometimes get you closer to what you want with far less computation.

105
00:11:31,438 --> 00:11:36,228
研究人员随后测试了这种共享几何结构是否真的有用。
The researchers then tested whether this shared geometry could actually be useful.

106
00:11:36,494 --> 00:11:41,124
他们首先使用分别训练好的图像编码器和文本编码器。
They started with an image encoder and a text encoder that had been trained separately.

107
00:11:41,550 --> 00:11:47,780
他们并没有联合重新训练它们，而是在它们的表征之间拟合了一个受限的几何映射。
Rather than jointly retraining them, they fitted a constrained geometric mapping between their representations.

108
00:11:48,302 --> 00:11:54,656
他们将此与在文本上训练的语言解码器结合，创建了一个图像标注系统，而且它奏效了。
They combined this with a language decoder trained on text to create an image captioning system, and it worked.

109
00:11:54,816 --> 00:12:02,148
他们最强的系统在 Coco 图像标注基准测试中获得了 0.838 的 CIDR 分数。
Their strongest system achieved a CIDR score of 0.838 on the Coco image captioning benchmark.

110
00:12:03,054 --> 00:12:11,056
这种映射确实需要一些成对的图像标注示例，但图像和文本编码器本身并没有联合训练。
The mapping did require some paired image caption examples, but the image and text encoders themselves weren't jointly trained.

111
00:12:11,150 --> 00:12:22,016
在另一个实验中，研究人员展示了未配对的图像和文本表征的几
何结构可以在没有对应成对数据的情况下帮助恢复它们的对齐。
And in a separate experiment, the researchers showed that the geometry of unpaired image
and text representations could help recover their alignment without corresponding pairs.

112
00:12:22,318 --> 00:12:30,916
更深层的含义是，独立训练的 AI 组件可能已经拥有兼容的内部结构。
The deeper implication is that independently trained AI components may already possess compatible internal structures.

113
00:12:36,096 --> 00:12:40,964
现在，这就是这项研究与 AI 更宏大愿景的联系点。
Now, this is where the research connects to a much bigger vision for AI.

114
00:12:41,294 --> 00:12:43,936
规划意味着预测场景将如何变化。
Planning means predicting how a scene will change.

115
00:12:44,206 --> 00:12:48,132
而这一直是 Yann LeCun 更广泛研究议程的核心。
And this has been central to Jan Lacoon's broader research agenda.

116
00:12:48,526 --> 00:12:59,012
其核心思想是构建能够学习环境紧凑表征的 AI 系统，然后预测这些表征在采取行动时如何变化。
The idea is to build AI systems that learn compact representations of their environment,
then predict how those representations change as actions are taken.

117
00:12:59,374 --> 00:13:01,668
这不仅仅是一个哲学上的提议。
And this isn't just a philosophical proposal.

118
00:13:02,062 --> 00:13:06,896
2025 年，Meta 的研究人员推出了 VGPA2。
In 2025, researchers at Meta introduced VGPA2.

119
00:13:07,278 --> 00:13:16,836
该系统使用超过一百万小时的视频进行预训练，学习视觉场景随时间展开的表征。
The system was pre-trained using more than a million hours of video, learning representations of how visual scenes unfold over time.

120
00:13:17,166 --> 00:13:27,140
然后，利用不到 62 小时的额外机器人视频，研究
人员开发了一个能够支持物理规划的动作条件模型。
Then, using fewer than 62 hours of additional robot videos, the researchers
developed an action condition model that could support physical planning.

121
00:13:27,438 --> 00:13:36,772
他们展示了机器手臂在系统未接受过特定任务训练的环境中，去够取、抓取并移动物体。
They demonstrated robot arms reaching for, grasping and moving objects
in environments where the system hadn't received task-specific training.

122
00:13:37,136 --> 00:13:47,684
虽然这离人类水平的物理智能还很遥远，但它代表
了迈向“不仅是识别世界”的机器的一个具体步骤。
Now, this is still far from human-level physical intelligence, but it
represents a concrete step towards machines that don't just recognize the world.

123
00:13:52,974 --> 00:13:57,316
到目前为止，这篇论文中几乎所有的内容都是关于“什么是不变的”。
So far, almost everything in this paper is about what stays the same.

124
00:13:57,710 --> 00:13:59,108
同一个场景的两个描述。
Two captions of one scene.

125
00:13:59,566 --> 00:14:01,536
从不同角度拍摄的两张照片。
Two photographs from different angles.

126
00:14:01,838 --> 00:14:07,456
良好的视角能教会模型保留观测中共同的部分，并忽略差异部分。
A good view teaches a model to keep what the observations share and ignore what differs.

127
00:14:07,630 --> 00:14:09,572
但 world models 需要其他的。
But a world model needs something else.

128
00:14:10,030 --> 00:14:11,652
它必须预测什么是变化的。
It has to predict what changes.

129
00:14:12,238 --> 00:14:19,204
推一下玻璃杯，它的位置会变，然后是速度，甚至最终可能是形状。
Push the glass, and its position changes, then its velocity, and perhaps eventually its shape.

130
00:14:19,456 --> 00:14:27,456
事实上，论文已经将视频片段的几个帧视为同一个场景的良好视角，这提出了一个难题。
In fact, the paper already treats several frames of video clip as good views of one scene, which raises a puzzle.

131
00:14:27,616 --> 00:14:33,156
如果模型学会把不同的时刻视为相同，那它怎么学会它们之间发生了什么呢？
If a model learns to treat different moments as the same, how does it learn what happened between them?

132
00:14:33,870 --> 00:14:35,748
这就是三个想法交汇的地方。
This is where the three ideas meet.

133
00:14:36,238 --> 00:14:42,020
柏拉图表征告诉我们，独立训练的模型何时会在场景结构上达成一致。
Platonic representations tell us when separately trained models will agree on the structure of a scene.

134
00:14:42,382 --> 00:14:47,876
causal representation learning 询问该结构的哪些部分会对干预做出反应。
Causal representation learning asks which parts of that structure respond to interventions.

135
00:14:48,398 --> 00:14:55,524
而像 V Jepa II 这样的 world models 则试图预测当采取行动时，这些表征如何移动。
And world models like V Jepa II try to predict how those representations move when an action is taken.

136
00:14:55,918 --> 00:14:58,404
良好的视角能否延伸到时间维度上？
Could a good view be extended through time?

137
00:14:58,894 --> 00:15:05,444
这样，独立训练的 world models 不仅能在场景包含的内容上达成一致，
还能在场景如何变化上达成一致？
So that independently trained world models agree, not just on what a scene contains, but on how it can change?

138
00:15:05,696 --> 00:15:10,336
作者指出视频和动作是自然的下一步。
The authors point to video and actions as natural next steps.

139
00:15:10,638 --> 00:15:16,068
将这些想法结合起来仍然是 AI 领域最有趣的开放问题之一。
Bringing these ideas together remains one of the most interesting open problems in AI.

140
00:15:21,902 --> 00:15:25,616
但这篇论文中还隐藏着一个更深层次的问题。
But there's an even deeper question hiding in this paper.

141
00:15:25,966 --> 00:15:32,516
为什么独立训练的智能，非得收敛到相同的现实表征上呢？
Why should independently trained intelligences converge on the same representation of reality at all?

142
00:15:33,006 --> 00:15:41,348
这是否意味着存在某些底层结构，任何足够强大的智能都有可能去发现？
Does it suggest there are certain underlying structures that any sufficiently capable intelligence will tend to discover?

143
00:15:41,934 --> 00:15:56,068
人类、基于图像训练的 AI、基于语言训练的 AI，甚至可能是某种完全不同的智能，
最终是否都能建立出数学上相关的相同环境表征？
Could a human, an AI trained on images, an AI train on language, and perhaps even a completely different kind of intelligence,
eventually develop mathematically related representations of the same environment?

144
00:15:56,558 --> 00:16:01,252
论文的理论提供了一个部分且出人意料的精确答案。
The paper's theory offers a partial and surprisingly precise answer.

145
00:16:01,678 --> 00:16:08,336
独立训练的模型达成一致的程度，取决于它们的训练是否将世界的相同状态视为相关。
Separately trained models agree to the extent that their training treats the same states of the world as related.

146
00:16:08,558 --> 00:16:12,676
留下来的是每种观测方式共有的结构。
What survives is the structure that every kind of observation shares.

147
00:16:13,166 --> 00:16:25,936
被剔除掉的是干扰项，即特定相机角度、特定词汇选择或特定感知方式的细节，
这意味着共同的图像并非世界直接赐予我们的。
What falls away is the nuisance, the details peculiar to one camera angle, one choice of words, one way of sensing,
which means the shared picture isn't simply handed to us by the world.

148
00:16:26,158 --> 00:16:29,028
它还取决于我们决定什么才算“相同”。
It also depends on what we decide counts as the same.

149
00:16:29,454 --> 00:16:38,500
那么，这些表征中哪些部分反映了世界的客观特征，哪些又是我们特定感官和学习目标的产物呢？
So, which parts of these representations reflect objective features of the world,
and which are artifacts of our particular senses and learning objectives?

150
00:16:39,342 --> 00:16:45,092
这是哲学中最古老的问题之一，现在作为关于机器的问题再次出现。
It's one of the oldest questions in philosophy, returning as a question about machines.

151
00:16:50,798 --> 00:17:00,384
多年来，我们一直在思考，如果给 AI 提供足够的参
数、足够的数据和足够的算力，它能变得多么智能。
For years, we've been asking how intelligent AI can become if we give it enough parameters, enough data, and enough computing power.

152
00:17:00,624 --> 00:17:02,784
这项研究提出了一个不同的问题。
This research raises a different question.

153
00:17:03,024 --> 00:17:09,220
如果通往通用智能的路径，同样取决于我们要求模型去学习什么呢？
What if the path to more general intelligence depends just as much on what we ask the model to learn?

154
00:17:09,806 --> 00:17:15,332
不仅仅是下一个词是什么，而是当描述发生变化时，哪些关系依然成立。
Not merely which word comes next, but which relationships remain true when the description changes.

155
00:17:15,950 --> 00:17:19,204
以及最终，哪些行动会导致哪些结果？
And eventually, which actions cause which outcomes?

156
00:17:19,662 --> 00:17:21,858
当然，还有一个更深层的问题。
And of course, there's a further question.

157
00:17:22,286 --> 00:17:30,116
如果我们最终构建出能够更准确地理解和预测世界的 AI 系统，这会让它们变得更安全吗？
If we eventually build AI systems that understand and predict the world far more accurately, does that make them safer?

158
00:17:30,542 --> 00:17:31,620
未必。
Not necessarily.

159
00:17:32,014 --> 00:17:36,068
了解世界如何运作，与知道该追求什么目标是两回事。
Knowing how the world works is different from knowing what goals to pursue.

160
00:17:36,398 --> 00:17:41,504
随着 AI 变得越来越强大，这是我们必须解决的另一个独立挑战。
And that's a separate challenge we'll have to solve as AI becomes more capable.

161
00:17:41,902 --> 00:17:45,956
Plato 想象了囚犯们把影子误认为是现实。
Plato imagined prisoners mistaking shadows for reality.

162
00:17:46,382 --> 00:17:53,412
今天，我们正在制造从它们自己的文本、图像、声音和数据等影子中学习的机器。
Today, we're building machines that learn from shadows of their own text, images, sounds, and data.

163
00:17:53,678 --> 00:17:59,812
而一个令人着迷的可能性是，它们可能会学会识别出这些影子背后的相同结构。
And the fascinating possibility is that they may learn to recognize the same structure behind them.

164
00:18:00,206 --> 00:18:04,964
问题不再仅仅是 AI 能吸收多少信息。
The question is no longer just how much information an AI can absorb.

165
00:18:05,230 --> 00:18:08,836
而是我们是否在教它去发现关于现实的正确事物。
It's whether we're teaching it to discover the right things about reality.

